  "body": {
    "id": "thm:quadratic-chain-flow",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "On any ordered grid \\(\\mathcal G_m\\), \\(f^x\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal N_x\\) if and only if it is feasible for the nested-survivor chain flow, equivalently if \\(\\sum_{i>k}f^x_{ij}\\le\\kappa^x_{kj}\\) for every \\(k,j\\) together with its two margins. The network has \\(O(m^2)\\) nodes and arcs when both time axes have \\(m\\) levels, every feasible flow lifts to a full law, and \\(h_m(c)\\) equals the stated node-potential dual for every signed cost \\(c\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\mathcal G_m",
      "f^x_{ij}",
      "\\mathcal N_x",
      "\\kappa^x_{kj}",
      "h_m(c)",
      "c^x_{ij}"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "thm:iff-capacity-projection",
      "def:chain-flow",
      "def:grid-support-dual",
      "ass:semicompeting-order",
      "ass:terminal-monotonicity",
      "lem:finite-lp-strong-duality"
    ],
    "justification": "The quadratic reduction makes exact joint-surface support computation possible and exposes the complete capacity constraints rather than hiding them in a four-time table.",
    "gap": "Russell and Ober-Reynolds provide generic latent-law programs and duals; neither source gives this nested chain, its complete suffix cuts, or the reconstruction of full semicompeting-risk laws.",
    "consumer": "It supplies an exact auditable algorithm for the finite-horizon grids used in the Comment et al. analysis and a certificate for every reported support endpoint.",
    "proof_tex": "The proof is a conservation reduction followed by finite linear-program duality.  Fix \\(x\\) and suppress it temporarily.  Index ordered death categories by \\(i=0,\\ldots,m\\), with the last category representing \\(\\infty\\).  A projected table has the required margins exactly when\n\\[\n \\sum_j f_{ij}=d_x(i),\\qquad \\sum_i f_{ij}=\\zeta_x(j).                \\tag{7}\n\\]\nOn the finite carrier, the survivor-capacity inequality for the singleton mark \\(\\{j\\}\\) is\n\\[\n \\sum_{i>k}f_{ij}\\leq\n \\mu_{1x}(D(1)>k,R(1)=j)=\\kappa^x_{kj}.                              \\tag{8}\n\\]\nSumming (8) over \\(j\\) in an arbitrary grid mark set proves every marked-capacity inequality; conversely, singleton sets recover (8).  Thus (7), (8), and nonnegativity are equivalent to \\(f^x\\in\\mathcal N_x\\) by the survivor-capacity definition.\n\nFor each mark \\(j\\), create chain nodes \\(v_{0j},\\ldots,v_{mj}\\), inject \\(\\zeta_x(j)\\) at \\(v_{0j}\\), put an exit of size \\(f_{ij}\\) from \\(v_{ij}\\) to death-demand node \\(i\\), and give the arc \\(v_{kj}\\to v_{k+1,j}\\) capacity \\(\\kappa^x_{kj}\\).  Conservation through the first \\(k+1\\) nodes forces the chain-arc load to equal\n\\[\n q_{kj}=\\zeta_x(j)-\\sum_{i\\leq k}f_{ij}\n =\\sum_{i>k}f_{ij}.                                                   \\tag{9}\n\\]\nEquations (7)--(8) therefore turn a feasible table into a feasible chain flow.  Conversely, the exit flows of a feasible network obey (7), and (9) with the arc capacities gives (8).  With \\(m\\) levels on each axis, the construction has \\(O(m^2)\\) chain nodes, exit arcs, and capacity arcs.  The full-law lift is the finite specialization of the proved capacity-projection theorem, whose hypotheses include terminal monotonicity and the within-arm semicompeting order.\n\nFor the support value in one cell, write the primal as\n\\[\n \\max_{f\\geq0}\\ \\sum_{i,j}c^x_{ij}f_{ij}\n\\]\nsubject to (7)--(8).  Put free multipliers \\(\\alpha_i,\\beta_j\\) on the two families of margin equalities and nonnegative multipliers \\(\\lambda_{kj}\\) on (8).  If\n\\[\n \\alpha_i+\\beta_j+\\sum_{k<i}\\lambda_{kj}\\geq c^x_{ij},\n \\qquad \\lambda_{kj}\\geq0,                                          \\tag{10}\n\\]\nthen every primal-feasible \\(f\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n \\sum_{i,j}c^x_{ij}f_{ij}\n &\\leq\\sum_{i,j}\\left(\\alpha_i+\\beta_j+\n        \\sum_{k<i}\\lambda_{kj}\\right)f_{ij}\\\\\n &=\\sum_i\\alpha_i d_x(i)+\\sum_j\\beta_j\\zeta_x(j)\n   +\\sum_{k,j}\\lambda_{kj}\\sum_{i>k}f_{ij}\\\\\n &\\leq\\sum_i\\alpha_i d_x(i)+\\sum_j\\beta_j\\zeta_x(j)\n   +\\sum_{k,j}\\lambda_{kj}\\kappa^x_{kj}.                            \\tag{11}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThis is weak duality for precisely the node-potential program in the grid-support definition.  The primal feasible set is a closed subset of a finite probability simplex, hence compact, and its signed objective is bounded.  The established finite LP strong-duality lemma makes (11) an equality at the optima and gives attainment.  Constraints do not share unknowns across \\(x\\), so the support of the product fiber is the sum of its cellwise supports, which is exactly \\(h_m(c)\\).",
